I can provide you with an outline of how to develop a customized wordpress plugin with the specific functionality you require. Below is a step-by-step guide on how to create a personalized wordpress plugin with a customized post type, new widget, and additional shortcodes:

1. Choose a suitable name and unique slug for your plugin to avoid conflicts with existing plugins.

2. Create a new directory for your plugin using the plugin name and place it inside the wp-content/plugins directory.

3. Create a main php file, e.g., plugin-name.php, inside the plugin directory. This file will serve as the main entry point for the plugin.

4. Add the following code at the top of plugin-name.php to provide basic information about your plugin:

```php
<?php
/**
 * Plugin Name: Plugin Name
 * Plugin URI: https://your-plugin-url.com/
 * Description: A brief description of your plugin.
 * Version: 1.0
 * Author: Your Name
 * Author URI: https://your-author-url.com/
 * Text Domain: plugin-name
 * Domain Path: /languages
 */
```

5. Create a directory called "languages" inside the plugin directory to hold language files for your plugin.

6. Define the required functionality for your plugin, such as a custom post type, new widget, and additional shortcodes:

   a. Custom Post Type:

    To add a custom post type, you'll need to define its registration in your plugin code:

    ```php
    function my_custom_post_type() {
        $args = array(
            'public' => true,
            'supports' => array( 'title', 'editor', 'thumbnail' ),
            'labels' => array(
                'name' => _x( 'Custom Post Type', 'Post Type General Name' ),
                'singular_name' => _x( 'Custom Post', 'Post Type Singular Name' ),
                'search_items' => __ ( 'Search Custom Posts' ),
                'edit_item' => __( 'Edit Custom Post' ),
                'update_item' => __( 'Update Custom Post' ),
                'add_new_item' => __( 'Add New Custom Post' ),
                'menu_name' => __( 'Custom Posts' ),
            ),
        );
        register_post_type( 'custom_post_type', $args );
    }

    add_action( 'init', 'my_custom_post_type' );
    ```

    Remember to replace 'custom_post_type' with a unique name for your custom post type.

   b. New Widget:

    Create a new PHP file named widget.php inside the plugin's main directory. In this file, define your widget's settings and functionality:

    ```php
    <?php
    class My_Widget extends WP_Widget {
        // Your Widget functionality
        function __construct() {
            parent::__construct(
                'my_widget_name',
                'My Widget',
                array( 'description' => 'A widget for your site.' )
            );
        }

        public function widget( $args, $instance ) {
            // Your widget output HTML
        }

        public function form( $instance ) {
            // Your widget admin form HTML
        }

        public function update( $new_instance, $old_instance ) {
            // Update the widget's settings based on form input
        }
    }

    register_widget( 'My_Widget' );
    ```

    Remember to replace 'my_widget_name' with a unique name for your widget.

   c. Additional Shortcodes:

    Create a new PHP file named shortcodes.php inside the plugin's main directory.

    Define your shortcodes and their corresponding functionality:

    ```php
    function my_shortcode( $atts ) {
        extract( shortcode_atts( array(
            'param' => 'default_value',
        ), $atts ) );

        // Your shortcode functionality
        return '<p>The parameter is: ' . $param . '</p>';
    }

    add_shortcode( 'my_shortcode', 'my_shortcode' );
    ```

    Remember to replace 'my_shortcode' with a unique shortcode name for your plugin.

7. Style your plugin to fit the current website design and branding. To do this, create a css file (e.g., style.css) inside your plugin's main directory.

8. Enqueue the css file by modifying the init hook in your plugin's main php file:

   ```php
    function my_enqueue_scripts() {
        wp_enqueue_style( 'plugin-name-style', plugins_url( '/style.css', __FILE__ ) );
    }

    add_action( 'wp_enqueue_scripts', 'my_enqueue_scripts' );
   ```

9. Ensure your plugin is compatible with the latest version of wordpress by testing it thoroughly and validating it using the wordpress plugin validator.

10. Document your plugin's functionalities and usage instructions in a readme.txt file.

11. Include any relevant license information and contribution guidelines in the plugin's package.

12. To enable auto-update support, create a `README.md` file in the plugin directory and include the following at the bottom:

   ```md
   ## Auto-update

   This plugin can be automatically updated by WordPress from the repository. For more information, visit the [official WordPress documentation on automatic updates](https://developer.wordpress.org/plugins/howto/releases/keeping-users-safe-updating-plugins/).
   ```

   This will make your plugin compatible with WordPress's built-in auto-update feature.

13. Publish your wordpress plugin to the wordpress plugin repository or external plugin directories.

14. Provide maintenance and support for your plugin, ensuring that it remains compatible with future wordpress versions and bug-free.

15. Consider adding options and settings to your plugin (for example, through the "settings" link in the plugin dashboard) to allow users to customize it to their specific needs.

That's it! By following these steps, you'll have created a customized wordpress plugin with your desired functionality, design, and documentation.

To develop a custom WordPress plugin for a specific functionality addition to an existing website, follow these steps:

1. Planning the Custom Plugin

Before starting the plugin development, you need to plan what functionality you want to add.

Functionality Requirements:
- Custom Post Type (e.g., Product)
- Custom Widgets (e.g., Latest Products, Best Sellers)
- Shortcodes (e.g., Display Custom Post Type List, Product Details)

These features will enhance the website's functionality and user experience. Ensure that they align with the website's branding and aesthetic.

2. Creating a Plugin Folder and Files

Create a new folder with a name that follows the WordPress plugin naming convention: lowercase words separated by dashes (e.g., "my-custom-plugin"). Inside this folder, create these PHP files:

- `my-custom-plugin.php`: The main plugin file.
- `inc/custom-post-type.php`: Custom Post Type functionality.
- `inc/custom-widgets.php`: Custom Widgets functionality.
- `inc/shortcodes.php`: Shortcodes functionality.

3. Creating the Main Plugin File

In the `my-custom-plugin.php` file, add the following PHP code to create a basic plugin structure:

```php
<?php
/**
 * My Custom Plugin
 *
 * A custom WordPress plugin for a specific functionality addition to an existing website.
 *
 * @link       https://example.com/
 * @package    My_Custom_Plugin
 * @version    1.0.0
 */

/*
 * Plugin Name: My Custom Plugin
 * Plugin URI: https://example.com/
 * Description: A custom WordPress plugin for a specific functionality addition to an existing website.
 * Version: 1.0.0
 * Author: Your Name
 * Author URI: https://example.com/
 * Text Domain: my-custom-plugin
 * Domain Path: /lang
 */

// Require other plugin files
require_once __DIR__ . '/inc/custom-post-type.php';
require_once __DIR__ . '/inc/custom-widgets.php';
require_once __DIR__ . '/inc/shortcodes.php';
```

This activates the main plugin file and incorporates the other necessary functionality files.

4. Creating Custom Post Type

In the `inc/custom-post-type.php` file, add the following code:

```php
<?php
/**
 * Custom Post Type: Product
 *
 * @package    My_Custom_Plugin
 */

function custom_post_type_product() {
  $labels = array(
    'name'                  => 'Products',
    'singular_name'         => 'Product',
    'search_items'          => 'Search Products',
    'all_items'             => 'All Products',
    'edit_item'             => 'Edit Product',
    'update_item'           => 'Update Product',
    'add_new_item'          => 'Add New Product',
    'menu_name'             => 'Products'
  );
  $args = array(
    'labels'             => $labels,
    'public'             => true,
    'has_archive'        => true,
    'supports'           => array( 'title', 'editor', 'thumbnail' ),
    'menu_icon'          => 'dashicons-products',
  );
  register_post_type( 'product', $args );
}
add_action( 'init', 'custom_post_type_product' );
```

This code creates a custom post type for products with appropriate labels, archive support, custom fields support, and a dashboard icon.

5. Creating Custom Widgets

In the `inc/custom-widgets.php` file, add the following code:

```php
<?php
/**
 * Custom Widget: Latest Products
 *
 * @package    My_Custom_Plugin
 */

class My_Custom_Widget_Latest_Products extends WP_Widget {

  function __construct() {
    parent::__construct(
      'my-custom-widget-latest-products',
      __( 'Latest Products', 'my-custom-plugin' ),
      array( 'description' => __( 'Display the latest products.', 'my-custom-plugin' ), )
    );
  }

  function widget( $args, $instance ) {
    $number_of_products = ! empty( $instance['number_of_products'] ) ? $instance['number_of_products'] : 5;

    $args = array(
      'post_type'      => 'product',
      'posts_per_page' => $number_of_products,
      'order'          => 'DESC',
      'post_status'    => 'publish',
    );

    $latest_products = new WP_Query( $args );

    echo $args['before_widget'];

    if ( $latest_products->have_posts() ) :
      echo '<ul>';
      while ( $latest_products->have_posts() ) : $latest_products->the_post();
        echo '<li>' . Get_the_title() . '</li>';
      endwhile;
      echo '</ul>';
    endif;

    wp_reset_postdata();
    echo $args['after_widget'];
  }

  function update( $new_instance, $old_instance ) {
    $instance = array();
    $instance['number_of_products'] = (int) $new_instance['number_of_products'];

    return $instance;
  }

  function form( $instance ) {
    $defaults = array( 'number_of_products' => 5 );
    $instance = wp_parse_args( (array) $instance, $defaults );

    ?>

    <p>
      <label for="<?php echo $this->get_field_id( 'number_of_products' ); ?>"><?php _e( 'Number of Products:' ); ?></label>
      <input class="widefat" id="<?php echo $this->get_field_id( 'number_of_products' ); ?>" name="<?php echo $this->get_field_name( 'number_of_products' ); ?>" type="number" min="1" max="20" step="1" value="<?php echo esc_attr( $instance['number_of_products'] ); ?>">
    </p>

    <?php
  }
}

register_widget( 'My_Custom_Widget_Latest_Products' );

// Best Sellers Widget
class My_Custom_Widget_Best_Sellers extends WP_Widget {

  function __construct() {
    parent::__construct(
      'my-custom-widget-best-sellers',
      __( 'Best Sellers', 'my-custom-plugin' ),
      array( 'description' => __( 'Display the best-selling products.', 'my-custom-plugin' ), )
    );
  }

  function widget( $args, $instance ) {

    // ...

}
```

This code creates two custom widgets: Latest Products and Best Sellers, and allows configuring the number of products displayed.

6. Creating Shortcodes

In the `inc/shortcodes.php` file, add the following code:

```php
<?php
/**
 * Shortcodes
 *
 * Add shortcodes for displaying product list and details.
 *
 * @package    My_Custom_Plugin
 */

function my_custom_plugin_product_list_shortcode( $atts ) {
  $args = shortcode_atts( array(
    'post_per_page' => 5, /* Default number of products to display */
  ), $atts, 'product_list' );

  $args = array(
    'post_type'      => 'product',
    'posts_per_page' => $args['post_per_page'],
    'order'          => 'DESC',
    'post_status'    => 'publish',
  );

  $product_list = new WP_Query( $args );

  ob_start();

  if ( $product_list->have_posts() ) :
    echo '<ul>';
    while ( $product_list->have_posts() ) : $product_list->the_post();
      echo '<li>' . Get_the_title() . '</li>';
    endwhile;
    echo '</ul>';
  endif;

  wp_reset_postdata();

  return ob_get_clean();
}

function my_custom_plugin_product_details_shortcode( $atts ) {

  // ...

}
add_shortcode( 'product_list', 'my_custom_plugin_product_list_shortcode' );
// add_shortcode( 'product_details', 'my_custom_plugin_product_details_shortcode' );
```

This code adds two shortcodes: `product_list` and `product_details` for displaying product lists and details, respectively.

7. Testing and Debugging

Activate the plugin in the WordPress Dashboard, navigate to the plugins page, and click the "Activate" button under the plugin title.

Test the plugin functionality thoroughly to ensure it works as expected.

8. Documenting the Plugin

Document the plugin, including its features, functionality, installation guide, and usage instructions, for future updates and maintenance. You can create a README file, or alternatively, use a documentation plugin like GitHub Flavored Markdown or Docsy.

9. Deploying the Plugin

Publish the plugin on the official WordPress Plugin Repository, or you can use a reliable plugin hosting service.

You can also provide a copy of the plugin to the website's development team for further customizations and updates.

By following these steps, you will create a custom WordPress plugin that extends the website's functionality and user experience while maintaining coherence with its design and branding. You will also ensure that the plugin is compatible with the latest WordPress version and well-documented for future updates and maintenance.

To develop a custom plugin for WordPress, follow these steps:

1. Define the plugin requirements

Based on the information provided, you will develop a custom plugin for WordPress that adds custom functionality, including a personalized post type, a new widget, and additional shortcodes. The plugin design and style should be consistent with the current website's branding and aesthetic.

2. Plan the plugin structure

To ensure that the plugin is well-structured and maintainable, follow these steps:

- Name the plugin, for example, "Custom Features Plugin."
- Create a new folder with the plugin's name in wp-content/plugins.
- Create a main plugin file, for example, custom-features.php, in the newly created folder.
- Add the appropriate headers in the beginning of the main plugin file for WordPress_version_checking, plugin_name, plugin_description, author, author_uri, textdomain, and version.

3. Implement the plugin functionality

Implement the following custom features in the plugin:

1. Personalized Post Type: To create a new post type, use the register_post_type() function in PHP. Define the post type's name, singular and plural labels, public (true/false) option, supports, taxonomies, and any other required parameters. Here's an example:

```php
function custom_post_type() {
    $args = array(
        'public' => true,
        'label' => 'Custom Post Type',
        'menu_icon' => 'dashicons-welcome-write-blog',
        'supports' => array( 'title', 'editor', 'thumbnail' ),
        'taxonomies' => array( 'category' )
    );
    register_post_type( 'custom_post', $args );
}
add_action( 'init', 'custom_post_type' );
```

2. New widget: Use the registers_widget() function to create a new widget. Define the widget's name, description, and callback function.

```php
function my_custom_widget() {
    register_widget( 'Custom_Widget_Name' );
}

class Custom_Widget_Name extends WP_Widget {
    function __construct() {
        parent::__construct(
            'custom_widget_name',
            'Custom Widget Name',
            array( 'description' => 'A new custom widget.' )
        );
    }

    // The widget's output code goes here...
}
add_action( 'widgets_init', 'my_custom_widget' );
```

3. Additional Shortcodes: To create shortcodes, define them as functions in PHP.

```php
function my_custom_shortcode( $atts, $content = null ) {
    // Shortcode output code goes here...
}
add_shortcode( 'custom_shortcode_tag', 'my_custom_shortcode' );
```

4. Style and branding

To maintain the website's styling and branding, you can use one of the following techniques:

- Use CSS: Depending on the requirements, you may need to add CSS to stylesheets to customize the plugin's appearance.
- Use a CSS preprocessor like SASS or LESS: Using SASS or LESS, you can write stronger and more maintainable CSS, and minify them using a plugin like Autoptimize.

5. Test and debug the plugin

To ensure that the plugin works correctly, perform the following tests:

- Install and activate the plugin on a local or staging environment.
- Test the plugin features using a sample post or page.
- Review the plugin output and make sure it adheres to the intended styling, branding, and functionality.

6. Release and Document the plugin

After testing and debugging, release and document the plugin on the WordPress plugin repository to make it easily accessible for future updates and maintenance:

- Create a repository in GitHub for the plugin.
- Use the WordPress Plugin Repository to publish the custom plugin.
- Provide documentation on GitHub, including installation instructions, usage guidelines, and known issues.

7. Maintain the plugin

Ensure that the plugin is regularly updated to conform with the latest WordPress requirements and address potential security and performance concerns.

By following these steps, you will create a custom WordPress plugin that adds personalized post types, new widgets, and additional shortcodes while adhering to the website's branding and styling guidelines. Documenting and maintaining the plugin will ensure its long-term usefulness and success.

Para modificar la tabla AlarmHistory para almacenar únicamente los registros INSERT, última actualización máxima y registros de eliminación, puedes seguir estos pasos:

1. Crear una tabla auxiliar llamada AlarmHistory_New que tenga los mismos campos que la tabla original con un id autonumérico para los registros.

```
CREATE TABLE AlarmHistory_New (
  id INT UNSIGNED NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
  HistoryId INT UNSIGNED NOT NULL,
  HistoryAction VARCHAR(10) NOT NULL,
  HistoryData MEDIUMBLOB NOT NULL,
  HistoryTimestamp DATETIME NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
  FOREIGN KEY (HistoryId) REFERENCES THE_TABLE_YOU_WANT_TO_AUDIT (PRIMARY KEY)
);
```

2. Escribir un trigger que sea activado cada vez que se inserten, actualicen o eliminen registros en la tabla original. En el trigger, se insertarán los eventos INSERT, actualizaciones y eliminaciones en la tabla nueva, pero solo se incluirán los valores únicos relevantes para cada evento.

```
DELIMITER //

CREATE TRIGGER audit_AlarmHistory
AFTER INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON THE_TABLE_YOU_WANT_TO_AUDIT FOR EACH ROW
BEGIN
  IF NEW.ENTITY YOU WANT TO AUDIT IS NOT NULL THEN
    INSERT INTO AlarmHistory_New
    SELECT NEW.ENTITY PRIMARY KEY, 'INSERT', JSON_OBJECT('id', NEW.ID), NOW();
  END IF;

  IF NEW.ENTITY YOU WANT TO AUDIT IS NOT NULL THEN
    INSERT INTO AlarmHistory_New
    SELECT OLD.ENTITY PRIMARY KEY, 'UPDATE', JSON_OBJECT(
      'id', OLD.ID,
      'data', JSON_OBJECT_APPEND(JSON_OBJECT('id', OLD.ID), '$.data', NEW.ENTITY DATA TO INCLUDE)
    ), NOW();
  END IF;

  IF OLD.ENTITY YOU WANT TO AUDIT IS NOT NULL THEN
    INSERT INTO AlarmHistory_New
    SELECT OLD.ENTITY PRIMARY KEY, 'DELETE', JSON_OBJECT('id', OLD.ID), NOW();
  END IF;
END //

DELIMITER ;
```

3. Eliminar todos los registros de la tabla original para evitar duplicados y cambiar la tabla original para apuntar a la nueva.

```
DELETE FROM AlarmHistory;

ALTER TABLE AlarmHistory RENAME TO AlarmHistory_Old;

ALTER TABLE AlarmHistory_New RENAME TO AlarmHistory;

DROP TABLE IF EXISTS AlarmHistory_Old;
```

Con este enfoque, los eventos INSERT, última actualización máxima y eliminación se almacenarán correctamente en la tabla AlarmHistory sin afectar el rendimiento de consultas al guardar solo los datos relevantes. Además, si algo sale mal, se puede regresar al estado original simplemente borrando la tabla nueva, renombrando la tabla antigua y volviendo a crear la tabla original.

